title: '[revive] pgas as storage deposit'
doc:
  - audience: Runtime Dev
    description: |
      ## Storage deposits backed by PGAS

      This PR adds a second payment backend for pallet-revive storage deposits: instead of
      always charging the user in native currency (DOT), a runtime can opt in to having
      deposits denominated in **PGAS**.

      ### New trait: `Deposit`

      `substrate/frame/revive/src/deposit_payment.rs`

      A new sealed `Deposit<T: Config>` trait abstracts over how storage deposits are
      charged, held, refunded, and collected. It has two implementations in-crate:

      - **`()`**: the default, charges and refunds the native currency. Identical to the
        existing pre-PR behavior.
      - **`PGasDeposit<Mutator, Holder, Id, RefundPercent>`**: the PGAS-backed backend.

      The trait is wired into `Config::Deposit` and called from `charge_deposit` /
      `refund_deposit` in place of the direct `T::Currency` calls that used to live there.

      ### New storage: `NativeDepositOf`

      `substrate/frame/revive/src/lib.rs`

      ```rust
      pub(crate) type NativeDepositOf<T: Config> = StorageDoubleMap<
          _, Identity, T::AccountId,
          Blake2_128Concat, T::AccountId,
          BalanceOf<T>, ValueQuery,
      >;
      ```

      Keyed `(holder_account, user) -> native_amount`. It records how much **native
      currency** a user has contributed to a given account's hold. The holder is either a
      contract (for storage deposits on contract accounts) or the pallet account (for
      code-upload deposits).

      It exists because in the mixed PGAS/DOT world, a user's refund cap needs to be
      tracked explicitly: we can't just look at what's on hold because PGAS and DOT are
      both on hold under the same reason. The map caps how much of a refund can come back
      as DOT; anything beyond that is settled in PGAS.

      ### `PGasDeposit<Mutator, Holder, Id, RefundPercent>`

      `substrate/frame/revive/src/deposit_payment.rs`

      Parameterized by four type parameters that the runtime wires up:

      - `Mutator: fungibles::Mutate` — the fungibles impl backing PGAS (e.g. `pallet-assets`).
      - `Holder: fungibles::MutateHold` — the holds backend (e.g. `pallet-assets-holder`).
      - `Id: Get<AssetId>` — the PGAS asset id on that fungibles instance.
      - `RefundPercent: Get<Perbill>` — the fraction of PGAS returned on refund/collect;
        the rest is burned.

      Charge semantics:

      - If the user has enough reducible PGAS, the full amount is paid in PGAS and no DOT
        is touched.
      - Otherwise the charge falls through to DOT, and the contribution is recorded in
        `NativeDepositOf` so it can be refunded as DOT later.

      Refund / collect semantics:

      - DOT is returned first, capped by `NativeDepositOf[holder][user]` (and by
        `Precision::BestEffort` on the actual DOT hold).
      - Any shortfall is taken from the PGAS hold. `RefundPercent` of that PGAS is
        transferred to the user's free balance; the remainder is burned.

      The `RefundPercent` burn is what prevents free-PGAS harvesting: a user can't deposit
      storage, release it, and walk away with an allowance they can spend on execution.

      ### Migration (v4)

      `substrate/frame/revive/src/migrations/v4.rs`

      Existing chains have storage deposits already held in DOT. The migration moves
      existing deposits over to the new accounting in two phases:

      **Phase 1** iterates `CodeInfoOf` and for each uploaded code credits the uploader's
      existing `CodeUploadDepositReserve` DOT hold into
      `NativeDepositOf[pallet_account][owner]`. This is needed so code-upload deposits can
      still be refunded in DOT once the PGAS path is live.

      **Phase 2** iterates `AccountInfoOf` and for each contract calls
      `Deposit::migrate_native_to_pgas`, which:

      1. Burns the native `StorageDepositReserve` hold from the contract.
      2. Mints the same amount of PGAS into the contract.
      3. Places the minted PGAS on hold under the same reason.

      Pre-PR DOT deposits weren't tracked per-contributor, so leaving them as DOT would
      let users harvest free DOT by refunding against deposits they never paid.
crates:
  - name: pallet-revive
    bump: major
  - name: pallet-pgas-allowance
    bump: major
  - name: asset-hub-westend-runtime
    bump: major
